Z||000008||Tigger||11-09-2000||04:23 PM||||Sounds real bad, I'd agree with the spun bearing with the knock and loss of power.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>It will start up after things cool down but you probably won't go far driving it.<P>Check the oil level, was it still ok on the stick?  Check the alternator belt to make sure it's good.  If it's too loose the waterpump won't work and the engine will build up heat in no time.  Just a couple things to check.  I'd look into the lemon laws again and get another opinion.<P>I seen the pic in your other post.  Nice looking, but this doesn't sound so good.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||11-09-2000||09:02 PM||||I'm sorry to hear of the problems with your GT.  I've been there done that.  I swapped out the dead (#5 cylinder) engine with 54000 miles on it in my 88 with a remanufacture 3.1.<P>That 3.1 is getting replace with a 3.4.<P>If the rest of the car body, frame, interior is in good shape, you can't find an more attractive car as a Fiero.  If the car has not been beat the suspension may still be good. (My 87 has the original suspesion still in it, but I've owned the car since new).<P>it is unfortunate that, you must have purchase the car with no warrenty at all, so you do end up screwd.  The fisrt car I bought had a 30 day warrenty and I got a water pump from the dealer.  It should have been a warning, because after the one repair under the warrenty, everything else I paid for out of pocket engine, transmission, shocks, carburator, drums, U joints, springs.  The stuff you do for your first car. (a '59 Plymouth convertible). Memories.. I could get up to 105 mph before the back barrels of the carb would open.<P>Sorry about my ramblings.  Your car was made 15 years ago, an engine overhaul can almost be considered a necessity at this time in its life.  There is lots of support in the land of Fieros and there are people who have reliable used stock 2.8 for reasonable prices.  If are fortunate as to live close to some one who does engine replacements, check out the costs, it may be less than you expect.  Or if you have more $, now is the time for more power. ||||reg||1
